# Initialize Pomegr reporting

## Scope

{{#codex}}Create or update only `.pomegr/signals.md`. Never edit `AGENTS.md`, Codex configuration, agent definitions, provider settings, or application code.

Inspect safe repository context before asking questions: project structure, package/build manifests, test and CI configuration, contributor guidance, names of `.codex/agents/` files, and any existing policy. Never inspect secrets, credentials, transcripts, prompts, responses, hook payloads, command-output archives, environment files, or MCP configuration values.{{/codex}}{{#claude}}Create or update `.pomegr/signals.md` and, only when needed, the `tools` allowlists of signal-owning agent definitions. Never edit `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, provider settings, or application code.

Inspect safe repository context before asking questions: project structure, package/build manifests, test and CI configuration, contributor guidance, `.claude/agents/*.md`, and any existing policy. Never inspect secrets, credentials, transcripts, prompts, responses, command-output archives, or environment files.{{/claude}}

## Inspect and validate

Resolve this plugin's `scripts/policy.mjs` relative to this file. When a policy exists, run `node <script> validate --cwd <repository-root>` and summarize its signals, delegated agents, and Session progress state. Preserve user-written table choices. {{#codex}}Treat version 6 as progress-disabled and ask before enabling progress during an upgrade.{{/codex}}{{#claude}}Report delegation warnings, restore drifted canonical sections from the template, and ask before enabling Session progress.{{/claude}}

## Configure

Read [the policy template](references/policy-template.md) and use it as the runtime policy structure.

- Configure only project-specific outcomes Pomegr cannot derive. Exclude generic lifecycle, context-size, Git, tool-count, approval, agent-liveness, {{#codex}}plan-task, and execution-task lifecycle{{/codex}}{{#claude}}and task-lifecycle{{/claude}} states.
- Ask only what is needed to settle each signal's scope, label, tone, trigger, and replacement or clearing condition. Session scope describes the overall goal; agent scope describes one agent's role or conclusion; task scope requires a {{#codex}}recognized{{/codex}}{{#claude}}known{{/claude}} execution-task ID.
- Session progress defaults to no and requires explicit confirmation to enable.
- For agent or task signals, add `Delegated agents` rows only for user-confirmed types that can reach a configured outcome. Use `*` only when every spawned type may report; otherwise leave delegation empty when the main session owns reporting.

{{#claude}}For each declared type, prepare any needed `tools` allowlist repair with the resolved namespace—typically `mcp__plugin_pomegr_pomegr__*`—or exact reporting suffixes; the user confirms them at the preview step. Do not add reporting prose to agent definitions: `PreToolUse` supplies the rows even when a definition is a thin wrapper around a canonical body elsewhere.

{{/claude}}Use the template's exact empty-section markers and table shapes. A delegation row may own only a scope with configured signals.

## Preview, write, and verify

- Preview the complete proposed Markdown or a focused diff{{#claude}} together with every agent-definition edit{{/claude}} and explain omitted duplicates.
- Obtain explicit user confirmation before writing.
{{#codex}}- Write only `.pomegr/signals.md` after confirmation, creating `.pomegr/` if needed. Preserve unrelated valid guidance instead of replacing the policy wholesale.{{/codex}}{{#claude}}- After confirmation, apply only the policy and allowlist changes, creating `.pomegr/` if needed. Preserve unrelated valid guidance instead of replacing the policy wholesale.{{/claude}}
- Re-run the validator and fix structural errors without changing approved semantics.{{#claude}} Report its warnings as delegation drift.{{/claude}}

